# The device tool configuration (GM-72 tier 2) — the file the OWNER edits.
#
# This is a COPY for reference. The live file is `tools.json` in the agent root, which is
# `%USERPROFILE%\.cbhr\endpoint-agent\tools.json` by default, written on first use by
# `node run-agent.js init` and never placed inside this repository.
#
# The rule, in one line: EFFECTIVE = SERVER CEILING ∩ THIS FILE, and this file may only NARROW.
#
# * A tool listed here that the server ceiling does not permit makes the WHOLE file invalid
#   (`ENDPOINT_DEVICE_CONFIG_INVALID`) and the agent refuses to start. It is NOT silently dropped:
#   GM-72 requires that a capability be visibly off "and by whose choice", and a silent drop would
#   leave you seeing a tool as on while the platform refuses it.
# * A tool inside the ceiling that you leave out here is simply OFF on this PC. The platform is told
#   so, and the panel shows it as your choice. Turning it back on happens HERE, on the PC — never in
#   the browser (the same principle as renewal, GM-12/R5).
# * Two names can never be enabled, whatever this file says: `track_ui_event` (internal handler,
#   always relay-denied 403) and `give_feedback_to_desktop_commander` (third-party egress, denied for
#   the pilot). The four approval-gated tools stay approval-gated even when listed.
#
# The shipped default below is the NARROWEST useful set: the three read-only tools this lab agent
# actually implements. Everything else in the ceiling would be refused at dispatch anyway with
# `ENDPOINT_TOOL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED`, so leaving them out keeps the effective set honest.
#
# Check what each side thinks with:  node run-agent.js tools

{
  "version": 1,
  "enabled": ["get_file_info", "list_directory", "read_file"]
}